The 4 Key Evaluation Domains
1. Cost-Efficient Architectural Design (~25%) [1, 2]
Focuses on evaluating architectural alternatives based on business value, unit economics, and ongoing operational costs rather than just raw resource reductions. [1, 2, 3]
Trade-off Engineering: Managing explicit balancing acts between speed, quality, performance, and infrastructure costs during product development.
Financial Constraints Early: Injecting architectural constraints directly into user templates and system pipelines.
Right-sizing Over Provisioning: Building workloads that dynamically adjust based on active metric demand. [1, 2, 3]
2. Technical Implementation of FOCUS Data Sets (~25%) [1]
Tests the engineer's ability to normalize and handle cross-cloud usage data. [1, 2]
The FOCUS Initiative: Understanding and processing FinOps Open Cost and Usage Specification (FOCUS) syntax to compare multi-cloud expenditures uniformly.
Bill Translation: Tracing container, compute, storage, or runtime metadata inside complex cloud billing logs.
Data Ingestion Paths: Normalizing unstructured billing data from public providers, private servers, and AI API microservices. [1, 2, 3, 4]
3. Automation & Engineering Feedback Loops (~25%) [1]
Emphasizes automated operational mechanisms to stop cost issues before they hit an invoice. [1]
Anomaly Management: Automating early detection scripts to spot runaway infrastructure builds, high-cost database tests, or runaway AI model training runs. [1, 2]
Automated Guardrails: Establishing Policy-as-Code checks directly inside continuous deployment pathways to reject over-provisioned or unapproved system layouts. [1, 2]
Tagging Controls: Designing strict automated tagging structures to trace resource consumption straight back to specific developer lines. [1, 2]
4. The FinOps Framework & Engineering Personas (~25%) [1]
Establishes the core processes necessary to communicate tech details clearly to financial groups. [1]
The 3 Lifecycle Phases: Interacting with business segments across the standard Inform, Optimize, and Operate structural loops. [1, 2, 3]
Partnership Models: Working via direct engineering contributions or targeted consulting models to implement shared cost metrics across large internal structures. [1, 2]
Unit Economics: Translating complex server metrics into concrete business data (e.g., measuring computing cost-per-active-user instead of aggregate cloud spend). [1, 2, 3]




